        "Alexandre Blais",
        "Andreas Wallraff",
        "Robert J. Schoelkopf"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit quantum electrodynamics brought cavity quantum optics into a solid-state platform, enabling controlled light-matter interaction with artificial atoms and opening a major experimental field.",
        "attribution": "The theory of circuit QED and its experimental realization are complementary contributions; Blais, Wallraff, and Schoelkopf represent those central steps.",
        "maturity": "The platform has been developed and reproduced across laboratories over many years, with broad influence on quantum optics and quantum information.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's later growth involved many groups, so the boundary between foundational contributions and subsequent development needs careful review."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexei Kitaev",
        "Andrew M. Steane",
        "Peter W. Shor"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed how quantum information can be protected against noise in principle, a foundational result that reshaped the prospects for quantum physics and computing.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane developed early quantum codes, while Kitaev's topological approach added a distinct and influential route; the proposed names capture key conceptual advances.",
        "maturity": "The theory is firmly established, and experimental demonstrations have advanced substantially, though large-scale fault-tolerant operation remains an unfinished goal.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The full history includes additional important contributors, and the committee would need to define whether it is recognizing the first codes or the broader framework."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Markus Greiner",
        "Peter Zoller"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattices turned ultracold atoms into controllable quantum many-body systems, making models of condensed-matter physics directly accessible to experiment.",
        "attribution": "Bloch and Greiner led landmark experimental realizations, while Zoller helped establish the theoretical vision for quantum simulation with trapped atoms.",
        "maturity": "The platform has produced reproducible phenomena and sustained progress over decades, though its relationship to simulating real materials remains an active scientific question.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The contributions of Cirac and other experimental groups may also warrant recognition depending on how the discovery is framed."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B023",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped-ion quantum computation connected quantum-information theory to a precise, controllable physical system and established a leading route to coherent quantum operations.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller proposed the influential ion-trap gate scheme; Blatt's experiments demonstrated and extended the approach in the laboratory.",
        "maturity": "The core control methods have been repeatedly validated and developed into increasingly capable processors, while scaling to fault tolerance remains challenging.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The long experimental history includes substantial contributions from other groups, so the award's scope would require careful definition."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eli Yablonovitch",
        "John D. Joannopoulos",
        "Sajeev John"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals established how periodic structures can control the propagation and emission of light, creating a broad and enduring area of optics.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John provided independent foundational proposals and demonstrations, while Joannopoulos made major theoretical contributions to the field.",
        "maturity": "The underlying physics is well established and has been realized in diverse systems, with continuing development in devices and applications.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery has broad contributions from both theory and experiment, and a narrow laureate selection may not reflect the field's full development."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Artur Ekert",
        "Charles H. Bennett",
        "Gilles Brassard"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum key distribution made quantum measurement principles operational in a striking new way and helped establish quantum information as an experimental discipline.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Brassard introduced a landmark protocol, while Ekert developed a distinct approach using entanglement and Bell inequalities.",
        "maturity": "The principles are experimentally mature and have been tested in increasingly capable communication systems.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The scientific distinction between the foundational physics and later communication engineering needs to remain clear."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Gwendal Fève",
        "Moty Heiblum"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Measuring fractional charge and probing statistics in quantum Hall systems provided direct experimental access to the unusual quasiparticles of a strongly correlated quantum state.",
        "attribution": "Fève represents electron quantum-optics measurements, while Heiblum represents influential interferometric studies; the proposed pair spans complementary experimental advances.",
        "maturity": "Fractional charge is robustly established, while some statistical interpretations and measurements remain demanding and system dependent.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The exact discovery being recognized and the relative roles of device development, charge measurements, and statistics experiments need clarification."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B019",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks brought exceptional precision and control to atomic measurement, enabling new tests of fundamental physics as well as advances in timekeeping.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye independently developed central approaches and demonstrated leading clock systems.",
        "maturity": "The technology is highly mature, with reproducible precision improvements and active use in fundamental measurements.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The committee should assess whether the principal recognition is for a basic physical discovery or for a transformative measurement technology."
        ]
      }
    }
